<!-- An example ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, the one these plugins were built around. Take the structure,
write your own lines: every line is loaded into every session and subagent. -->
# Global rules

Budget: **≤1300 words.** Loaded into every session and subagent. A rule belongs here only if no
`local/<repo>-*.md`, skill, domain doc or hook can hold it. Adding means deleting.

## ABSOLUTE

- **NEVER write to production.** MySQL is `ro-mysql` only, BigQuery `bqro` only; raw clients,
  `bq query` and pymysql one-liners are denied. No INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/DDL, no "just fix the
  row": read, report, let the user run the fix. Topology: `skills/debug/references/`.
- **STAGING = any tunnel or login path named `*staging*`/`*stg*`** (`ro-mysql --tunnels`; ro-mysql
  labels every query). Its ids are fabricated: never carry one to prod or answer a prod/RCA
  question from it, and say "staging" when you use it. Every other tunnel is prod.
- **No mocks, patches or stubs in backend/integration tests.** Use real fixtures, DB and existing
  factories; no inline seed data. A suite that is mock-based by design (a frontend Jest suite
  its `<repo>-rules.md` names) stays so.
- **Ask before destructive ops** (force-push, `reset --hard`, dropping data, deleting branches or
  worktrees), even in auto mode.
- **Run a representative local test subset before pushing**, chosen by who consumes the OUTCOME:
  when a failing path starts succeeding, grep what depended on the old result.

## Where things live

- This file and `~/.claude/local/<repo>-*.md` are mine and authoritative. A repo's `CLAUDE.md`,
  `AGENTS.md` and `.claude/` belong to other devs and don't load in repos listed in
  `local/no-project-settings`; their rules are mirrored in `<repo>-invariants.md`.
- `CONTEXT-MAP.md`, `context/*.md` and `docs/adr/` are domain docs, not instructions. Navigate
  root map → `src/lib*/CONTEXT-MAP.md` → subject, from the docs-rollup worktree when one exists.
- **What to read in a repo:** `<repo>-invariants.md` before writing code; `<repo>-testing.md`
  only if you will run tests; `<repo>-rules.md` for conventions, PR labels and ROLLOUT.

## Subagents

- **You get this file but not the SessionStart injection.** Read the docs above for your task.
  House style is the `conventions` skill (custom agents without the Skill tool Read
  `skills/conventions/SKILL.md`).
- **You cannot spawn agents.** If asked for a review that needs them, say the main session must.
- **Spawning one? Restate the rules its task depends on** (test command, domain-doc entry point,
  what to read). Use `worker`, not general-purpose, for multi-step work: one per PR or phase,
  started from a brief file; never resume one above 300K context. Second opinion: `adversary`.

## Code

- Read the `conventions` skill before writing Python or a query.
- **Probe a lib from a scratch dir:** `uv run --no-project --with <pkg>`. Inside a project, a bare
  `uv run --with` rebuilds `.venv`.
- **`py_compile` proves syntax, not imports.** Boot the module or run one fast test.
- Comments only where they clarify something non-obvious.

## Reading data

Prod questions go through the **`debug` skill**: its reading traps and answer format apply
before you present any figure.

## Git / PR

- **Finish the task; don't check in mid-way.** Standing authorization to commit, push and update
  PRs, including red CI, review comments and broken tests. Stop only for ABSOLUTE or a real
  blocker, and say so loudly.
- **Asking blocks me for minutes; assuming costs one line.** Ask only when either path would be
  unsafe or waste the work. Otherwise state the default and carry on. Don't end on "Want me
  to…"; do it.
- **One commit per push.** Before a PR exists, amend the unpushed commit. Once a PR is open,
  never amend or rebase: commit once, right before the push. Never force-push. Hooks enforce.
- Subject ≤72 chars, imperative; body says why. Author: `GIT_AUTHOR` in `local/kit.env`, else
  git config; `bash-guards` prints the required trailers. Share PRs and issues as full URLs.
- **Ticket work happens in `EnterWorktree` `<TICKET>-<short-title>`.** Take the base from where
  recent PRs landed (`gh pr list --state merged --json baseRefName`); a lib may land elsewhere.
- **One PR per ticket** unless told otherwise. Handoffs go to `~/.claude/handoffs/<TICKET>.md`.
- **Diff three-dot against `origin/<base>...HEAD`.** A stale local branch of the same name
  silently wins. Re-derive ahead/behind counts yourself.
- **Verify `HEAD` before reading a branch you didn't check out** (`git worktree list` when it
  disagrees). A wrong checkout returns old code with no error.
- **Diff every path before staging**, never `git add -A`: a path may hold someone else's edits.
- **Never merge in a shared worktree:** `git worktree add --detach <tmp> origin/<branch>`, merge,
  verify, push. Non-fast-forward on staging: fetch and merge, never force.
- Match the repo's PR template. PR-ready labels and ROLLOUT: `<repo>-rules.md`.
- **The final message leads with "You need to:"** when there are deploy-order constraints or
  manual steps. Never bury them in a list.

## CI

Diagnosing a red run? Read `~/.claude/local/ci-rules.md` first.

## Planning, docs and refactors

- **Plans are a PRD with sequential numbered steps** (Context, Goals, Non-goals, Steps,
  Verification, Open questions). No phases or time estimates.
- **One entry point with declarative config beats N specific ones.** Add a mode to an existing
  skill, extend a module before adding a package.
- **Hook the state WRITER, not one caller.** Grep the status value and enumerate every writer.
- **Refactor: `grep -rn '<removed string>' tests/`** for every assert, error string and method
  moved or deleted. A dropped assert is a contract change.
- **Change one variable per experiment.**
- **When an early step produces findings, re-read the remaining steps.**
- **Figures in a deliverable come from a script, not transcription.** Durable docs describe
  shape, not counts.
- **Before claiming "no such mechanism", grep the domain noun** (`outbound_manifest`) and `docs/`.
- **Verify semantics at the source.** A field means what its owner does with it; a gate mirrors
  what the consumer enforces.
- **Writing a test:** scope queries to its own entity, distrust a test that never passed in CI,
  and prove a regression test fails on the pre-fix code. More in `<repo>-testing.md`.
- **Using a skill: stay in its native format.**
- **Artifacts: light mode only**, legible body face, and a diagram wherever it beats prose.

## Review

- **Follow the `review-trigger` Stop hook's instruction** for agents and tiering.
- **Verify an inherited finding by its mechanism**, against prod or the base commit. A corrected
  detail narrows the claim; it doesn't retract it.
- **A reviewer's note is intent.** If the literal proposal is wrong, build the strongest version
  that isn't, or say why it's a separate PR.

## Tooling

- **Context is the cost.** Every byte is re-read on every later call. Bound every read to the
  range you lack; give read-heavy side tasks to a subagent.
- **Edit files with Edit/Write**, never a shell in-place rewrite (it bypasses the format and
  review hooks). Scripts are for mechanical multi-file sweeps, on bytes.
- **In a worktree-isolated session** the harness refuses heredocs, loops, `;`/`&&` chains and any
  path outside the worktree, including `git -C <main checkout>`. Write a script to the
  scratchpad and run `python3 <file>`.
- **Logs and scratch files go to the scratchpad dir** named in the system prompt; `/tmp` and
  `$TMPDIR` are refused.
- **Bash caps at 600s.** Wait on background work with `until <check>; do sleep 10; done` under
  `run_in_background: true`, then stop and let the notification wake you.
- **Never `git worktree remove` inline** (it outruns the cap). Leave it to `claude-gc`.
- **`gh` needs the repo as cwd.**
- **Trace call chains with `LSP incomingCalls`/`outgoingCalls`**; the consumer that breaks is
  often two hops out. `workspaceSymbol` follows the session root.
- **Scratch worktrees don't survive across days.** Read a known file before reusing one.
- **Editing a hook or shell script? Read `local/claude-kit-rules.md` first.**
- **MCP tools bind at session start.**
- **After two failed patches to a system you can't read**, add observability or hand it back.
